 "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40eb77e9-b368-50ce-9757-312b75ebf09b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494093} :answer-field/id #uuid "d961e701-76e1-559b-8cba-a78f269ed6ef" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22250596-3a65-57bc-811f-38c308a05ad0" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-4{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}^{′}(x)=-6x$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-4{x}^{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-4{x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =-8x \\\\  & \\ne -6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=-4{x}^{2}$ and ${y}^{′}=-8x$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -8x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2(-4{x}^{2})}{x} \\\\ -8x & =-8x\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=-4{x}^{2}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494088 :